@@ -0,0 +1,482 @@
+{
+ "cells": [
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "8d4457db-98eb-4cbe-a45e-581f64c92899",
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "source": [
+    "# BitBIRCH"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "f8d080e0-317b-40fb-876d-1cc3f91503dd",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "This notebook acts as a tutorial for the usage of the new refinement methods available in [BitBIRCH](https://pubs.rsc.org/en/content/articlelanding/2025/dd/d5dd00030k).\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Please reach out to one of the following with any questions or concerns. \n",
+    "\n",
+    "Ramon Alain Miranda Quintana: <ramirandaq@gmail.com>, <quintana@chem.ufl.edu>\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Kate Huddleston: <kdavis2@chem.ufl.edu>\n",
+    "\n",
+    "Kenneth Lopez Perez: <klopezperez@chem.ufl.edu>\n",
+    "                            "
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "817f9b0e",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Please install the bitbirch editable package using the following commands in your terminal (individually):\n",
+    "```\n",
+    "git clone https://github.com/mqcomplab/bitbirch.git\n",
+    "cd bitbirch\n",
+    "pip install -e .\n",
+    "```"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 19,
+   "id": "fb7202cf-4af2-4fda-8782-dc957cbbd389",
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "## Imports \n",
+    "import numpy as np\n",
+    "from rdkit import Chem\n",
+    "import bitbirch.bitbirch as bb\n",
+    "import bitbirch.plotting_utils as plotting_utils\n",
+    "import pandas as pd"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "00dbb066-4da8-44d7-9cf6-9df6588e2d01",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "## Setting up our data"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "ebd45035-670a-4c37-8833-d20f17b8fbb7",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "First let's take our SMILES strings and compute our molecular fingerprints. With BitBIRCH, it is **REQUIRED** that the molecular representations are binary fingerprints of dtype int64. "
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "9d3fb975-70fe-4d1e-a281-8b2fdb0ff34c",
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "source": [
+    "Below we are reading the .smi file and outputting some np.array of SMILES strings. "
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 23,
+   "id": "383bef0c-2b46-4d78-b929-4814bfe18c43",
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Number of smiles:  64086\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "with open('../../data/chembl_33_np.smi', 'r') as f:\n",
+    "    smiles = np.array([line.strip() for line in f])\n",
+    "print(\"Number of smiles: \", smiles.shape[0])"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "1463851d-7aee-4737-a84e-3460444cf424",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "We then use [RDKIT](https://github.com/rdkit/rdkit) to generate our binary fingerprints."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 77,
+   "id": "65bde165-7545-4bf8-9752-7e87b4721369",
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "mols = [Chem.MolFromSmiles(S) for S in smiles]\n",
+    "fps = np.array([Chem.RDKFingerprint(mol) for mol in mols])  "
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 3,
+   "id": "9fb08709",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "fps = np.load('/home/kenneth/Documents/birch/bitbirch_sandbox/data/chembl_33_np.npy', mmap_mode='r')"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "843070b5",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Check that the number of fingerprints and smiles match"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 9,
+   "id": "d1eaf79a",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Number of mols and smiles is the same:  True\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "n_mols = len(fps)\n",
+    "n_smiles = len(smiles)\n",
+    "\n",
+    "print(\"Number of mols and smiles is the same: \", n_mols == n_smiles)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "21c919ef-cc31-49c4-94ed-d38796282340",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Now let's check the quality of the generated fingerprints. As stated about we need them to be binary of dtype int64. "
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 4,
+   "id": "5efb1c5e-5741-4763-9023-cdf07809c630",
+   "metadata": {
+    "tags": []
+   },
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Bits are binary: True\n",
+      "Data type is int64: True\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "print(f'Bits are binary: {np.all(np.isin(fps, [0, 1]))}')\n",
+    "print(f'Data type is int64: {fps.dtype == np.int64 or fps.dtype == np.float64}')"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "782862fa",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Now we are going to set a threshold and branching factor for our clustering"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 5,
+   "id": "8ea13ec3",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "branching_factor = 50\n",
+    "threshold = 0.65"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "ebd1f2b9",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "According to our observations in the refinement paper, we determined that the best way was to do initially diameter birch. We are going to fit now the fingerprints."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 6,
+   "id": "5e0b0c23",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "text/plain": [
+       "<bitbirch.bitbirch.BitBirch at 0x775403543a00>"
+      ]
+     },
+     "execution_count": 6,
+     "metadata": {},
+     "output_type": "execute_result"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "bb.set_merge('diameter')\n",
+    "brc = bb.BitBirch(threshold=threshold, branching_factor=branching_factor)\n",
+    "brc.fit(fps)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 7,
+   "id": "583b2d66",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Saved cluster metrics for diameter to data/diameter_cluster_metrics.csv\n"
+     ]
+    },
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "image/png": "",
+      "text/plain": [
+       "<Figure size 1000x500 with 2 Axes>"
+      ]
+     },
+     "metadata": {},
+     "output_type": "display_data"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "plotting_utils.save_metrics_to_csv(brc, fps, smiles, method='diameter')\n",
+    "plotting_utils.init_plot('diameter')"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "c3811aa7",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "### Refining our results\n",
+    "\n",
+    "You can notice above that there's a big cluster with a numerous number of scaffolds. If your data, does not present a large cluster with high number of scaffolds, your clustering is done, no need to refine.\n",
+    "For this we are going to create a new tree passing at first the well-defined clusters and then the molecules in the biggest/messy cluster.\n"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 7,
+   "id": "e02957fc",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "text/plain": [
+       "<bitbirch.bitbirch.BitBirch at 0x775401fd6b90>"
+      ]
+     },
+     "execution_count": 7,
+     "metadata": {},
+     "output_type": "execute_result"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "# Prepare the data from the first tree to refit\n",
+    "rest, big = brc.prepare_data_BFs(fps)\n",
+    "\n",
+    "# Delete the initial tree to save memory\n",
+    "#del brc\n",
+    "\n",
+    "# Create a new tree, this time with the data from the first tree and using tolerance merging and a higher threshold\n",
+    "bb.set_merge('tolerance', 0.00)\n",
+    "threshold = 0.70\n",
+    "brc_refined = bb.BitBirch(threshold=threshold, branching_factor=branching_factor)\n",
+    "\n",
+    "# Fit the new tree, notice that for this we are using fit_BFs, since we took the BitFeatures from the first tree\n",
+    "brc_refined.fit_BFs(rest)\n",
+    "brc_refined.fit_BFs(big)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 10,
+   "id": "17dae8a6",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Saved cluster metrics for refined to data/refined_cluster_metrics.csv\n"
+     ]
+    },
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "image/png": "",
+      "text/plain": [
+       "<Figure size 1000x500 with 2 Axes>"
+      ]
+     },
+     "metadata": {},
+     "output_type": "display_data"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "plotting_utils.save_metrics_to_csv(brc_refined, fps, smiles, method='refined')\n",
+    "plotting_utils.init_plot('refined')"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "b21c98f8",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "In case you want to visually inspect the cluster, with the following function you can generate images with the structres in each cluster. You just need not input the bitbirch object, the smiles, and the cluster number you want to plot. The function will generate multiple images with 30 molecules each, since it is not convenient to have all in one."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 27,
+   "id": "e530b162",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Number of molecules: 8\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "plotting_utils.mol_visualization(smiles, brc_refined, cluster=1243)"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "758503ea",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "If a spatial visualization is wanted, we can create a tSNE plot"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 31,
+   "id": "a69f5dff",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "data": {
+      "image/png": "",
+      "text/plain": [
+       "<Figure size 800x600 with 2 Axes>"
+      ]
+     },
+     "metadata": {},
+     "output_type": "display_data"
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "plotting_utils.tsne_plot(brc_refined, fps, \"refined\", title=\"refined\")"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "840a4622",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "Let's check the number of generated clusters and number of clusters above 10 molecules"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 30,
+   "id": "161c6177",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [
+    {
+     "name": "stdout",
+     "output_type": "stream",
+     "text": [
+      "Number of clusters:  14653\n",
+      "Number of clusters with more than 10 molecules:  979\n"
+     ]
+    }
+   ],
+   "source": [
+    "mol_ids = brc_refined.get_cluster_mol_ids()\n",
+    "print(\"Number of clusters: \", len(mol_ids))\n",
+    "\n",
+    "# Get the clusters with more than 10 molecules\n",
+    "clusters = [cluster for cluster in mol_ids if len(cluster) > 10]\n",
+    "print(\"Number of clusters with more than 10 molecules: \", len(clusters))"
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "markdown",
+   "id": "d10d4203",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "source": [
+    "If we are content with our clustering we can do the final assignments for our data."
+   ]
+  },
+  {
+   "cell_type": "code",
+   "execution_count": 24,
+   "id": "0c6d08ce",
+   "metadata": {},
+   "outputs": [],
+   "source": [
+    "assignments = brc_refined.get_assignments(n_mols=n_mols)\n",
+    "\n",
+    "# Save assignments to our smiles\n",
+    "df = pd.DataFrame({'smiles': smiles, 'assignments': assignments})\n",
+    "df.to_csv('smiles_assignments.csv', index=False)"
+   ]
+  }
